      "**Materials and form**\n\nCoral stone (often cut from reefs) was the primary building material. Mosques typically had flat or shallow-domed roofs, minimal minarets, and ornate wooden doors. The Great Mosque of Kilwa (11th–15th century) is one of the oldest surviving Swahili mosques and part of a World Heritage site.",
      "**Trade and culture**\n\nSwahili cities were hubs of Indian Ocean trade; mosque design sometimes reflected Persian and Indian motifs. Today, restored and active Swahili mosques remain in Kenya, Tanzania, and the Comoros. Browse Swahili and East African mosques on MosqueList.",
