      "MosqueList is free, open source software. The project is released under the **MIT License**, which means you can use, copy, modify, and distribute the code—including for commercial projects—as long as you keep the license and copyright notice. We want the app to belong to the community and to stay available for anyone building a mosque bucket list or exploring Islamic heritage.",
      "**How to fork the repository**\n\nForking gives you your own copy of MosqueList on GitHub so you can experiment or contribute back. Go to [github.com/codingshot/mosquelist](https://github.com/codingshot/mosquelist) and click **Fork**. Clone your fork to your machine (e.g. `git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/mosquelist.git`), then install dependencies with `npm install` and start the dev server with `npm run dev`. You can now change the code or data locally and push to your fork. When you're ready to propose a change to the main project, open a **Pull Request** from your fork to the original repo.",
      "**Ways to contribute**\n\nYou don't need to be a developer to help. **Report issues or suggest mosques:** Open an issue on GitHub for a new mosque suggestion, a data correction (wrong capacity, date, or address), or a bug. Use the in-app **Contribute** page (linked from the footer at `/contributing`) for the JSON schema, examples, and a form that generates mosque entries. **Add or edit mosque data:** Most contributions are edits to `src/data/mosques.json`—adding a new mosque or fixing an existing one. Each mosque needs a unique `id`, name, location, country, capacity, established date, and at least one source (e.g. Wikipedia or official site). The Contribute page explains the fields and where to add data. **Code and docs:** Fix bugs, improve accessibility, add tests, or update documentation. Follow the contribution guidelines in the repo: create a branch, make your changes, run `npm run lint` and `npm run test`, then open a Pull Request with a clear description. **Share and spread the word:** Tell others about MosqueList—Muslims planning spiritual travel, students of Islamic architecture, or travelers looking for visitor-friendly mosques. Every user and every contribution helps the project grow.",
